# Commercial S10.1 readiness and rollback diagnosis

Date: 2026-09-02

## Scope

This diagnosis covers the failed, public-SFW-only S10.1 activation attempt. It
does not authorize Adult media, AVS, payment, publishing, creator access,
controlled beta or production.

## Observed failure

The canonical S10.1 WMS process reached its ready event, but the one-shot local
health gate was launched in the same second as the `Type=simple` service. Its
first loopback request raced the listener and returned
`S10_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT_RED`, which the controller correctly converted to
`LOCAL_HEALTH_RED` and a fail-closed rollback.

During rollback, the three legacy services were also started as `Type=simple`
units and the external legacy health assertion followed immediately. The
listener was not ready yet, Nginx returned 502, and rollback reported
`LEGACY_PUBLIC_HEALTH_RED`. The legacy processes subsequently became healthy
without another mutation: S7, S8 Telegram, S8 landing and Nginx were active
with zero restarts, and the local legacy health endpoint returned HTTP 200.

DNS was not the cause of this attempt. The controller recorded
`S10_1_WMS_DNS_GREEN` before installation.

## Correction

The controller now waits up to 30 seconds for the exact loopback health
endpoint after starting the WMS listener and after starting the legacy S7
listener. Only then may it run the full health envelope. Exhausting the bound
still fails closed; no second process start or restart is performed.

## Risk and rollback

The change only makes readiness ordering deterministic. It does not weaken any
health assertion, product boundary or credential check. The verified
pre-S10.1 backup at
`/opt/tu1nz_repos/backups/commercial-s8-public-telegram/20260902T045348Z-pre-s10-1-local-install`
remains the server rollback basis. Reverting this commit restores the previous
controller implementation.

## Follow-up evidence

The bounded readiness correction proved that the listener itself became ready.
The next local gate then exposed a separate deterministic mismatch:
`S10_PUBLIC_BRAND_RED`. The landing renders the approved slogan in uppercase,
while the health script compared mixed-case bytes. Brand and legacy-name checks
now normalize the rendered page before comparison; the required words and all
legacy-brand rejection rules remain unchanged.

The same run also showed that rollback restored the exact source-era S8/S9
configuration, but the verifier compared those files to the S10.1 target
hashes. Separate immutable source hashes are now bound for rollback, including
the S8 copy. Target installation continues to use the existing target hashes.

## 2026-09-02 third fail-closed activation

The third controlled attempt stopped at `LOCAL_HEALTH_RED` with the safe
diagnostic `S10_GROWTH_BOUNDARY_RED`.  The loopback WMS was ready; the local
gate was incorrectly comparing the still-live S9 source contract/database
boundary (Telegram channel and audience seeding disabled) with the future S10
target boundary.  No public cutover occurred.

The automatic rollback restored the source application, source S8/S9
contracts, S7, S8 Telegram, S8 landing, nginx and both legacy health endpoints.
It then stopped fail-closed before restoring the recorded S9 timers because
`require_s9_restored_green` incorrectly required the target S9 channel state.
The recorded timers therefore remained disabled pending the next controlled
maintenance window.  There was no adult-media, AVS, payment, creator-beta or
publishing activation.

The corrective contract now:

- validates the source S9 boundary during the loopback-only local gate;
- hash-binds and applies the already-reviewed 0026/0027 S9 Telegram activation
  migrations only inside the public cutover transaction window;
- reverses those migrations before restoring the source application; and
- validates the actual source S9 channel and privilege boundary during
  rollback before restoring the recorded timer state.

The privilege boundary is tri-state: exactly zero grants is the source state,
all five required column grants is the target state, and every partial grant
set is rejected as drift.  A partial privilege mutation can therefore never
be mistaken for a clean rollback.

All four S9 transition migrations are streamed from the pinned S10.1 target
commit rather than read from the mutable application worktree.  The target
commit tree and the individual migration SHA-256 are checked immediately
before PostgreSQL receives the migration.  This keeps both forward activation
and recovery independent of the current checkout.

## 2026-09-02 Telegram profile transaction

The next controlled attempt passed backup verification, preflight, local WMS
readiness and TLS, then stopped at the pre-growth health gate.  Telegram
authenticated the expected immutable bot ID and username, but its provider
profile still carried the legacy display name.  The target contract correctly
rejected that state as `S8_TELEGRAM_IDENTITY_MISMATCH`; no growth timer or
observation was activated.  The automatic recovery restored the source
application and the public legacy services.

The S10.1 controller previously installed the new WMS bot contract but omitted
the existing bounded `--configure-only` reconciliation before starting the
worker.  It now stops the worker, applies the exact target display name,
description, short description and commands through that idempotent path, and
only then starts and probes the worker.  The call has a three-attempt bounded
retry and suppresses provider output so credentials cannot enter evidence.

Because Telegram profile state is external, rollback now performs the inverse
transaction: after restoring the source application and configuration it
reconciles the source profile before restarting the source S8 worker.  A
failed forward or inverse reconciliation remains fail-closed.  Bot username,
token, channel rights and every Adult/AVS/payment/publishing boundary are
unchanged.

## 2026-09-02 systemd start-limit recovery

The next controlled attempt reached the target S8 start after the Telegram
profile reconciliation, but systemd rejected the start because earlier
diagnostic attempts were still counted inside the unit's 15-minute rate-limit
window.  The rollback restored the source files and repository provenance, but
the same stale counters then rejected the otherwise-correct legacy starts.
The separately prepared baseline recovery cleared only those unit counters and
restored S7, S8 landing, S8 Telegram, nginx and public health to green.

The controller now clears only the explicitly named unit failed/start-limit
state immediately before each already-authorized bounded start.  It does not
add retries, start new services or weaken health checks.  Target activation and
rollback are therefore independent of earlier diagnostic counters while all
existing fail-closed gates remain intact.

The first execution of that correction exposed systemd's separate behavior
for a newly installed one-shot unit that has a valid unit file but has never
been loaded: `reset-failed` reports the unit as not loaded even though the
following start is valid.  Start-limit clearing now accepts only that exact
combination (`LoadState=not-found` plus a regular unit file at the approved
systemd path) as an expected no-op.  A missing file or every other unexpected
load state still fails closed before the start.

The following controlled attempt proved a second, equally safe systemd no-op
case: after `daemon-reload`, the same never-started unit is already
`LoadState=loaded`, while `reset-failed` can still report that no failed unit is
known. The guard now accepts only an inactive unit whose regular file exists at
the approved path and whose `FragmentPath` is exactly that path. For the
pre-load case it still requires `LoadState=not-found`, an empty `FragmentPath`
and the same regular file. Failed, activating, deactivating, missing, generated,
transient or path-divergent units remain fail-closed.

## 2026-09-02 legacy publication lease reconciliation

The next controlled attempt reached the WMS content seed and then stopped as
`S9_LEGACY_CAMPAIGN_IN_FLIGHT`. One legacy SFW Telegram queue row remained in
the first-attempt `PUBLISHING` state. Replaying it would risk a duplicate
public message because Telegram does not accept the internal idempotency key.
The campaign transition therefore correctly committed no partial WMS seed and
the controller restored the public S7/S8 baseline.

The public Telegram channel contains exactly one post whose normalized text
has the deterministic SHA-256 expected for that exact legacy content item and
whose provider post ID is `2`. The correction adds a credential-free,
read-only public-evidence verifier and invokes the application's existing
fail-closed reconciliation transaction through a hardened, loopback-only
one-shot service. Reconciliation is allowed only while the legacy publication
gate is closed, only for one exact first-attempt lease, and only after the
public post digest matches. It records the already-public result; it cannot
send a new Telegram message. A missing, additional or different lease, page,
post ID or text digest stops activation.

Rollback health also exposed a provenance mismatch: the server intentionally
retains the legacy S9 health helper while Control contains the channel-enabled
target helper. S10.1 now leaves the legacy helper untouched, uses a separate
hash-bound target launcher through an activation-only systemd drop-in, and
removes that drop-in during rollback. The pre-arm verifier is bound to the
observed legacy helper hash. This preserves exact source behavior after
rollback and exact target behavior after cutover without replacing the shared
baseline executable.

## 2026-09-02 final redirect readiness

The complete public-SFW observation finished `GREEN` after 7,203 seconds and
25 successful samples. The first final `.de` redirect assertion ran
immediately after the successful Nginx reload and observed the previous
redirect for one transient probe. The installed configuration already had the
exact final hash and both `.de` hosts returned the required permanent redirect
on the following read-only checks. Re-running the idempotent finalizer then
completed green without changing any product boundary.

The controller now gives only that exact post-reload redirect up to 30 seconds
to become externally observable. Every other status, destination or exhausted
bound still fails closed. This changes neither TLS, Nginx content, application
state nor authorization scope.
